former Secretary of State Condoleezza  Rice is drawing on her government  experience to help other leaders  understand today's political landscape  around the world her new book is called  political risk how businesses and  organizations can anticipate global  insecurity  it is co-authored by fellow Stanford  professor Amy zegart  we welcome Secretary Rice back to studio  57 good morning good morning let's talk  first about North Korea president Trump  has described North Korea's leader  yesterday as very open and very  straightforward so far  do you believe Kim jong-un will ever  abandon his nuclear weapons program  well the North Koreans have made a lot  of promises about denuclearization I  think I may have been the last Secretary  of State to actually negotiate with  North Koreans back in 2007 2008  2000-2009 and they're under pressure as  they were then they have a tendency when  they're under pressure under sanctions  to come to the table to go on a kind of  charm offensive in fact I think  secretary Tillison actually former  secretary Tillison actually deserves a  lot of credit for putting together a  pretty tough isolation campaign one of  the things that wasn't noticed about 20  countries expelled North Korean workers  that meant hard currency going back to  the regime was cut off the regime was  starting to run out of military parts so  they come to the table now the question  is are they serious I would have only  three admonitions for the administration  the first is this could be an opening  this is the first time we've dealt this  directly I was impressed with Secretary  of Secretary Pompeo then CIA director  Pompeo getting to Pyongyang and none of  us knew that's pretty amazing and then  so we're negotiating directly that's  good but the three admonitions don't  forget that there are other interested  parties for instance Japan has very  important interests here secondly take  it one step at a time it's fine to talk  about a peace treaty on the Korean  Peninsula in fact we talked about a  peace treaty that should be the ultimate  goal but Gulf step-by-step make sure  there's good verification of everything  the North Koreans are doing and keep  your eye on the prize of being  because what we want to do is stop them  short of threatening the American  homeland and then finally remember the  nature of this regime this is a regime  that murdered an American citizen just a  year ago this is a regime where the  leader ganda are killed his half-brother  in Malaysia using VX gas it's a brutal  regime a secretive regime I have no  problem with negotiating with them but  beware we talked about the inspections  regime with the Iran deal what for you  is the most important part of an  inspections regime if there is a deal  one day to make sure that they don't  cheat and and what should people be  looking for well the most important  thing about an inspection regime with a  country that you know has cheated in the  past is what I would call snap  inspections meaning that you can go  anywhere any time that you wish to send  inspectors there these regimes are very  good at for instance cleaning up a site  after they've been notified that you  want to look at that site and so they  tend to list their known sites but hide  other sites and so you have to be able  to go anywhere anytime but let me just  say getting inspectors on the ground can  make a difference and I hope that one of  the first steps in these North Korean  negotiations would be to get inspectors  International Atomic Energy Agency  expect errs on the ground because our  intelligence about North Korea is not  very good being on the ground with  experienced people you pick up things  that you wouldn't be able to pick up  through other means we talked about the  president and the Iran deal and the fact  that he may potentially withdraw from  that how could how might that affect  negotiations with North Korea well some  have said the North Koreans will then  think that America's not trustworthy  right but this would be a little bit the  the pot calling the kettle black right I  mean the North Koreans saying Americans  aren't trustworthy I'm not too worried  about that so you don't think I I don't  really think that this would be an issue  the North Koreans are going to negotiate  if they negotiate because it's in their  interest to do so  because they're under pressure  what has happened with Iran I think  isn't the issue it might be a little bit  of an object lesson though for Kim  jong-un that we negotiated with Moammar  Qaddafi and Gadhafi didn't end up so  well like I think that maybe more in his  memory bank than what we do with Iran in  your book you call the the landscape the  most complex security environment in  modern history how have those political  risks evolved since you were Secretary  of State and as President Trump made it  that are worse well there's there's no  doubt that the sources of political risk  are multiplying if one thinks about the  old days we worried about if you're a  business they're gonna expropriate my  property Cuba or they're like not so  much a risk anymore but who would have  thought that one of the big risks would  be the use of social media platforms for  a great power behaving badly the  Russians to interfere in our elections  that's one of the new ways in which  technology matters when you think about  the risk that comes from somebody simply  having a cellphone and seeing a bad  incident in your store when you think  about long supply chains and what trade  wars or tariffs could do to supply  chains that for instance are in Canada  or in Mexico the day after 9/11 we close  the border with Canada within three days  nobody could make a car because the  supply chain was in Canada when you  start threatening trade wars you  threaten things like supply chains so I  think the sources of political risk are  multiplying anyway but anything that  adds to that uncertainty like talk  around tariffs and trade wars certainly  increases that uncertainty for  businesses well that brings us then to  this president usually in the in the  global order after the second world war  the United States was the stable slow  moving rock upon which people could  depend we have a highly improvisational  president who gets some credit for North  Korea from and he knows that critics  alike however that sense of  improvisation trade where here maybe not  a trade war there you talked about  alliance management there have been some  bruised feelings with alliances how much  has he contributed to this  risk and how do you how do you manage  against that well there's certainly a  sense that there's volatility in the  American political landscape that is  unusual in our history but I wouldn't  overstate it to the point that well this  president is therefore the source of  political risk I think that's unfair you  know the fact is these sources of  political risk have been multiplying for  some time now  when terrorism became an issue it meant  that as we heartened airports for  instance if you were in the hospitality  industry all of a sudden you were a soft  target the tear the risks that come from  cyber security have been multiplying for  some time so yes it's the United States  is always better for people when we're  stable and steady and so forth but these  sources of political risk have been  multiplying for a long time I wonder as  someone who has been in the war room how  critical is it for a president to be  surrounded with or have access to  advisors who have opposing points of  view it's absolutely critical that the  president here opposing points of view  it can be very easy in that room the  Situation Room for everyone to start  reinforcing each other and so the  president needs to be able to hear no  mr. president that's that's not right  he needs also to be able to hear it and  away from his advisors that it doesn't  appear with all due respect to the media  on the front page of the New York Times  well dude is that a concern for you  given this president and the structure  of his career well it's a concern for me  because it looks to me that some of the  constraints some of the barriers that  people felt about being in the White  House and keeping that within the cone  that some of that has broken down but  that said this is a different president  he's never been in government before  it's the first time that we've actually  elected somebody whose first job in  government was president United States  and so we had to expect that there would  be some differences he still has many  very good people around him but I hope  going into this very important set of  negotiations with North Korea that  you're going to see a disciplined  process because the  Koreans are masters at taking advantage  of loopholes and weaknesses and so I'm  able Eve that Secretary of pompei looks  like he's going about it in the right  way but this is going to take real  discipline ask you one question about  political risk and this kind of this  world in which everybody's  interconnected and things are more  threatening than ever make the case or  not for diplomacy in such a world where  you can have problems break out at any  moment is that connectivity should  America reach out to the world or  withdraw America has to reach out to the  world  we have been since the end of World War  two the guardians of a system that has  served everybody really very well a  prosperous international economy that  was not zero-sum game before World War  Two in World War one people were  engaging in beggar-thy-neighbor trading  policies currency manipulation conflict  over resources we came along and said  you know this can be a positive sum game  where everybody wins lifted hundreds of  millions of people out of poverty the  international economy worked we  advocated for democracy we believed in a  democratic peace we rebuilt Germany and  Japan as great democracies believing  that they could be strong as long as  they were democratic it works splendidly  and we protected it with American  military power and American attention to  values and I hope that we're going to  hear more about that because it's worked  pretty well for the world and it's  worked pretty well for us I know it  hasn't worked for every single corner of  America and there the president is right  we have to look at what's happening to  our fellow citizens who can't read have  anymore can't have any more kids who go  to college get lots of debt and no  usable job skills you can't have any  more 35 year olds who can't be retrained  so he is right to focus on that aspect  of America that people have to be able  to benefit from the American dream but  my goodness I can't think of a better  guardian of an international system  built on our values and our interest  than the United States of America here's  you to just about the state of the white  house because you worked with george w  bush  closely and that one house there were  reports last night that the president's  chief of staff has called the president  an idiot  that his former Secretary of State at  the time Tillerson who you know well  called him a [ __ ] what does that say  about how this White House is being  operated well it says something about  people who would go out and say those  things about conversations that they did  or did not hear within the White House  that's that breakdown of discipline it's  been coming for a while you know it's  not just this administration that's had  damaging leaks but you know the the  level of comedy in a White House you  know these jobs are hard enough you  don't need to get up in the morning and  read in the newspaper that somebody is  saying something about the president  whatever you may think about the  president I've had my disagreements with  this president but he and I have many  but he is President of the United States  let's respect the office because it's  extremely important that the the White  House function as a unit secretary rice  thank you so much the new book political  risk how businesses and organizations  can anticipate global insecurity is on  sale today

